(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-15"] = {"phy-12-15-m01":{"idea":"Thomson's model is the plum pudding model: positive charge spread out, with electrons stuck in it.","steps":["The positive charge is spread evenly through the atom.","The electrons are embedded in it like seeds in a watermelon.","The atom as a whole is neutral."],"others":"A hollow shell, a negative sphere with protons, and a tiny nucleus with orbits are other pictures. The last one is Rutherford's model.","trap":"Thomson: spread-out positive. Rutherford: tiny nucleus.","answer":"a sphere of positive charge with electrons embedded in it"},"phy-12-15-m02":{"idea":"If most alpha particles go straight through, the atom must be mostly empty.","steps":["If charge and mass were spread through the atom, every alpha particle would be deflected.","Most were not deflected at all.","So most of the atom is empty space, with its charge in a very small region."],"others":"A volume filled with electrons or with positive charge would deflect many particles. Alpha particles are charged, so the last option is wrong.","trap":"Straight through means empty space.","answer":"most of the volume of an atom is empty space"},"phy-12-15-m03":{"idea":"A few alpha particles bouncing back means a tiny, heavy, positive core.","steps":["Only a very strong electric field can turn back a fast alpha particle.","That needs a lot of positive charge packed into a tiny space.","So the positive charge and most of the mass are in a very small nucleus."],"others":"Heavier electrons would not turn alpha particles around. A uniform positive charge could not do it either. A regular arrangement of atoms is not the cause.","trap":"Large-angle scattering points to the nucleus.","answer":"the positive charge and most of the mass are in a very small nucleus"},"phy-12-15-m04":{"idea":"Rutherford's model fails because orbiting electrons should radiate and fall in.","steps":["An electron going round a circle is accelerating.","Classical physics says an accelerating charge radiates energy.","It would lose energy and spiral into the nucleus very quickly, but atoms are stable."],"others":"The nucleus can hold positive charge. Alpha scattering is explained well. Electrons do not go straight away.","trap":"Bohr fixed this with stationary orbits.","answer":"the orbiting electrons would radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus"},"phy-12-15-m05":{"idea":"Each series of hydrogen lines ends at a different level.","steps":["Lyman series ends at n = 1, in the ultraviolet.","Balmer series ends at n = 2, mostly in the visible region.","Paschen series ends at n = 3, in the infrared."],"others":"Ending at n = 3 is Paschen. Balmer lines are not all in the ultraviolet. Ending at n = 1 is Lyman.","trap":"Lyman 1, Balmer 2, Paschen 3.","answer":"n = 2, and the lines are mostly in the visible region"},"phy-12-15-m06":{"idea":"Bohr allowed only orbits with angular momentum in whole units of h/2π.","steps":["Bohr's second postulate: m v r = n h/2π.","n = 1, 2, 3 and so on.","The electron does not radiate in these orbits."],"others":"Any value would not give fixed orbits. A multiple of h or of 2π/h gives the wrong units or size.","trap":"h over 2π is the unit.","answer":"an integral multiple of h/2π"},"phy-12-15-m07":{"idea":"Energy levels of hydrogen are E_n = −13.6/n² eV.","steps":["For n = 2, E = −13.6/4.","E = −3.4 eV.","The negative sign shows the electron is bound."],"others":"−13.6 eV is the ground state. −6.8 eV uses n² wrongly. +3.4 eV has the wrong sign.","trap":"Divide by n squared, keep the minus sign.","answer":"−3.4 eV"},"phy-12-15-m08":{"idea":"Ionisation energy from a level is the negative of its energy.","steps":["In the n = 2 state E = −3.4 eV.","To free the electron, its energy must go up to zero.","So 3.4 eV is needed."],"others":"13.6 eV is for the ground state. 10.2 eV is the energy for the jump from n = 1 to n = 2. 1.51 eV is the energy to ionise from n = 3.","trap":"Match the energy to the starting level.","answer":"3.4 eV"},"phy-12-15-m09":{"idea":"Bohr orbit radii grow as n squared.","steps":["r_n = a₀ n².","For n = 3, r = 9 a₀.","That is about 0.48 nm."],"others":"3 times uses n, not n². 27 times uses n³. 6 times does not follow the formula.","trap":"Radius goes as n²; energy goes as 1/n².","answer":"9 times the Bohr radius"},"phy-12-15-m10":{"idea":"The shortest wavelength comes from the biggest energy jump.","steps":["Jump 4 → 3: 13.6(1/9 − 1/16) = 0.66 eV.","Jump 3 → 2: 13.6(1/4 − 1/9) = 1.89 eV.","Jump 2 → 1: 13.6(1 − 1/4) = 10.2 eV, the largest.","Wavelength is inversely proportional to energy, so 2 → 1 gives the shortest."],"others":"The other jumps have smaller energy differences. The jumps are not equal.","trap":"Big energy, short wavelength.","answer":"the jump 2 → 1, which has the largest energy difference"},"phy-12-15-m11":{"idea":"Count each pair of levels once.","steps":["From n = 4 the possible jumps are 4→3, 4→2, 4→1, 3→2, 3→1 and 2→1.","The number is n(n − 1)/2 = 4 × 3/2.","So there are 6 lines."],"others":"10 is for n = 5. 4 and 3 count only some of the jumps.","trap":"Formula: n(n − 1)/2.","answer":"6"},"phy-12-15-m12":{"idea":"Isotopes have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.","steps":["Same atomic number Z, so the same element.","Different mass numbers A, because the neutrons differ.","These are isotopes."],"others":"Isotones have the same neutron number. Isomers are the same nucleus in different energy states. Isobars share the mass number.","trap":"Isotopes: same Z. Isobars: same A. Isotones: same N.","answer":"isotopes"},"phy-12-15-m13":{"idea":"Nuclear density is almost the same for all nuclei.","steps":["R ∝ A^(1/3), so the volume ∝ R³ ∝ A.","The mass is also ∝ A.","So the density is constant, about 2 × 10¹⁷ kg/m³."],"others":"It does not rise or fall with mass number. It is not zero for even mass numbers.","trap":"Volume and mass both go as A.","answer":"nearly the same for all nuclei, as R is proportional to A^(1/3)"},"phy-12-15-m14":{"idea":"A nucleus is lighter than its separate parts, and the missing mass is its binding energy.","steps":["The mass of a nucleus is less than the sum of its nucleons.","The difference is the mass defect Δm.","It appears as the binding energy: E = Δm c²."],"others":"The decay constant, packing fraction and mass number are different things.","trap":"Binding energy = mass defect × c².","answer":"the mass defect, which appears as the binding energy"},"phy-12-15-m15":{"idea":"Energy is released when nuclei move towards the peak of the binding energy curve.","steps":["The curve peaks near A = 56 (iron).","Heavy nuclei split (fission) and move towards the peak.","Very light nuclei join (fusion) and also move towards the peak. Both release energy."],"others":"Very light nuclei splitting would move away from the peak. A nucleus near iron splitting needs energy. Heavy nuclei joining would move away from the peak.","trap":"Heavy: split. Light: join.","answer":"a very heavy nucleus splits, or very light nuclei join"},"phy-12-15-m16":{"idea":"An alpha particle carries away 2 protons and 2 neutrons.","steps":["An alpha particle is a helium nucleus with Z = 2 and A = 4.","So the parent loses 2 units of atomic number.","It loses 4 units of mass number."],"others":"A decrease of 2 in A is wrong. Z decreasing by 1 with A unchanged is electron capture or positron decay. Z decreasing by 4 does not match.","trap":"Alpha: Z down 2, A down 4.","answer":"Z decreases by 2, and A decreases by 4"},"phy-12-15-m17":{"idea":"In beta-minus decay a neutron turns into a proton.","steps":["A neutron changes into a proton, with an electron and an antineutrino emitted.","The number of nucleons stays the same, so A is unchanged.","The number of protons goes up by one, so Z increases by 1."],"others":"Z decreasing by 1 is beta-plus. The other two change A, which a beta decay does not.","trap":"Beta minus: Z up 1, A same.","answer":"Z increases by 1, and A is unchanged"},"phy-12-15-m18":{"idea":"Gamma decay is a nucleus dropping to a lower energy level and giving out a photon.","steps":["The excited nucleus goes to a lower energy level, like an atom does.","It emits a high-energy photon.","The photon has no charge or mass number, so A and Z do not change."],"others":"Z increasing by 1 describes beta decay. An electron emission is beta. A helium nucleus emission is alpha decay.","trap":"Gamma decay changes the energy, not the identity.","answer":"emits a photon, and its A and Z are unchanged"},"phy-12-15-m19":{"idea":"Half-life is the time to fall to half: T½ = 0.693/λ.","steps":["At t = T½, N = N₀/2.","So e^(−λT) = 1/2.","Then λT = ln 2 = 0.693, so T½ = 0.693/λ."],"others":"1/λ is the mean life. 0.693λ and λ/0.693 have the wrong dependence on λ.","trap":"Mean life is longer than half-life.","answer":"0.693/λ"},"phy-12-15-m20":{"idea":"Count the number of half-lives, then halve that many times.","steps":["12 days is 12/4 = 3 half-lives.","Each half-life halves the number.","Fraction = (1/2)³ = 1/8."],"others":"1/12 and 1/6 come from dividing by days. 1/3 is not a power of one half.","trap":"Multiply halves; do not subtract.","answer":"1/8"},"phy-12-15-m21":{"idea":"The becquerel is one decay per second.","steps":["Activity is the number of decays per second.","One becquerel is one decay per second.","The older unit, the curie, is 3.7 × 10¹⁰ Bq."],"others":"3.7 × 10¹⁰ per second is the curie. Per half-life and per hour are not the SI definition.","trap":"1 Ci = 3.7 × 10¹⁰ Bq.","answer":"one disintegration per second"},"phy-12-15-m22":{"idea":"A moderator slows neutrons so they cause more fission.","steps":["Fast neutrons from fission are not captured easily by U-235.","A moderator, such as heavy water or graphite, slows them down.","Slow neutrons are captured more easily and cause more fission."],"others":"Absorbing neutrons is the job of control rods. Removing heat is done by the coolant. A moderator does not supply neutrons.","trap":"Moderator slows, control rods absorb, coolant carries heat.","answer":"slow down the fast neutrons so that they can cause more fission"},"phy-12-15-m23":{"idea":"Fusion needs high temperature to beat the repulsion between nuclei.","steps":["Nuclei are positive, so they repel each other.","They must get close enough for the strong nuclear force to act.","That needs fast motion, so temperatures of millions of kelvin."],"others":"Electrons attracting nuclei and nuclei attracting each other are wrong. Neutrons do not need high temperature for this.","trap":"Like charges repel, so heat is needed.","answer":"the nuclei, which repel each other, must be brought very close together"},"phy-12-15-m24":{"idea":"Mean life is longer than half-life by a factor of about 1.44.","steps":["Mean life τ = 1/λ.","Half-life T½ = 0.693/λ.","So τ = T½/0.693, about 1.44 T½."],"others":"τ = 0.693 T½ would make τ smaller. τ = T½/2 and τ = T½ do not follow from the definitions.","trap":"τ is bigger than T½.","answer":"τ = T½/0.693, so τ is greater than T½"},"phy-12-15-n01":{"idea":"Hydrogen lines follow 1/λ = R(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²).","steps":["Balmer second line: n₁ = 2, n₂ = 4.","1/λ = 1.097 × 10⁷ × (1/4 − 1/16) = 1.097 × 10⁷ × 3/16 = 2.057 × 10⁶ m⁻¹.","λ = 4.862 × 10⁻⁷ m = 486 nm."],"trap":"Use the lower level as n₁.","answer":"486 nm"},"phy-12-15-n02":{"idea":"The series limit of Lyman is the jump from infinity to n = 1.","steps":["1/λ = R(1/1² − 1/∞²) = R.","λ = 1/(1.097 × 10⁷) = 9.12 × 10⁻⁸ m.","λ = 91 nm."],"trap":"This photon carries the full 13.6 eV.","answer":"91 nm"},"phy-12-15-n03":{"idea":"For hydrogen-like ions the orbit radius is r_n = a₀ n²/Z.","steps":["n = 2 and Z = 3.","r = 0.053 × 4/3.","r = 0.0707 nm, which is 0.071 nm."],"trap":"A larger Z pulls the orbit in.","answer":"0.071 nm"},"phy-12-15-n04":{"idea":"Energy of a hydrogen-like ion is E_n = −13.6 Z²/n² eV.","steps":["Z² = 9 and n² = 4.","E = −13.6 × 9/4 = −30.6 eV.","The magnitude is 30.6 eV."],"trap":"The negative sign means bound.","answer":"30.6 eV"},"phy-12-15-n05":{"idea":"The number of lines from level n is n(n − 1)/2.","steps":["The atom starts from the level n = 5.","The number of lines is n(n − 1)/2 = 5 × 4/2 = 10.","So there are 10 different lines."],"trap":"Each pair of levels gives one line.","answer":"10"},"phy-12-15-n06":{"idea":"Nuclear radius grows as the cube root of the mass number.","steps":["R = R₀ A^(1/3).","125^(1/3) = 5.","R = 1.2 × 5 = 6.0 fm."],"trap":"Cube root, not square root.","answer":"6.0 fm"},"phy-12-15-n07":{"idea":"Binding energy = mass defect × 931.5 MeV per u.","steps":["Mass of parts = 2 × 1.007825 + 2 × 1.008665 = 4.032980 u.","Mass defect = 4.032980 − 4.002603 = 0.030377 u.","E = 0.030377 × 931.5 = 28.30 MeV."],"trap":"Use atomic masses with hydrogen atoms so electron masses cancel.","answer":"28.30 MeV"},"phy-12-15-n08":{"idea":"The fraction left is (1/2) raised to the number of half-lives.","steps":["20 days/8 days = 2.5 half-lives.","Fraction = (1/2)^2.5.","= 0.1768."],"trap":"Half-lives need not be whole numbers.","answer":"0.1768"},"phy-12-15-n09":{"idea":"Activity is A = λN.","steps":["λ = 0.693/(10 × 3600 s) = 1.925 × 10⁻⁵ s⁻¹.","A = λ N = 1.925 × 10⁻⁵ × 4.0 × 10²⁰.","A = 7.70 × 10¹⁵ Bq."],"trap":"Change hours to seconds first.","answer":"7.70 (in units of 10¹⁵ Bq)"},"phy-12-15-n10":{"idea":"Mean life is half-life divided by 0.693.","steps":["τ = T½/0.693.","τ = 6.93/0.693.","τ = 10.0 hours."],"trap":"Mean life is longer than half-life.","answer":"10.0 hours"},"phy-12-15-n11":{"idea":"Energy released is the mass lost times 931.5 MeV per u.","steps":["Δm = 238.050788 − 234.043601 − 4.002603 = 0.004584 u.","Q = 0.004584 × 931.5.","Q = 4.27 MeV."],"trap":"The energy is shared by the alpha particle and the thorium nucleus.","answer":"4.27 MeV"},"phy-12-15-n12":{"idea":"Total energy is the number of nuclei times the energy per fission.","steps":["Number of nuclei = (1000/235) × 6.022 × 10²³ = 2.563 × 10²⁴.","Energy per fission = 200 MeV = 200 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ J = 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ J.","E = 2.563 × 10²⁴ × 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ = 8.20 × 10¹³ J."],"trap":"1 MeV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ J.","answer":"8.20 (in units of 10¹³ J)"},"phy-12-15-n13":{"idea":"First Lyman line: n = 2 to n = 1.","steps":["1/λ = R(1 − 1/4) = (3/4) × 1.097 × 10⁷ = 8.228 × 10⁶ m⁻¹.","λ = 1.215 × 10⁻⁷ m.","λ = 122 nm."],"trap":"Photon energy is 10.2 eV.","answer":"122 nm"}};
